this is two more Hussein standing in for  reviewer David Walt goose combat really  last longer than a handful of rounds in  the u.s. XCOM in each encounter chimera  squad kicks in the door dives headfirst  into an intense firefight and then walks  out through a pile of bodies a minute or  two later dusting themselves down a  little scarred perhaps but eager to do  it all again in the very next mission X  comes turn-based tactical combat now  comes in a condensed form relieved of  extraneous matter and for the most part  all the better for it assuming the human  resistance triumphed in X come to the  eponymous chimera squad is a special  task force composed of human and alien  soldiers that also serve as a symbol of  a newfound cooperation between species  dissidents remain however and so when  the mayor of city 31 is killed in a  terrorist attack it's up to the squad to  track down those responsible the stakes  feel a lot lower than the global  emergencies gripping previous X comms  yet the smallest scale of the situation  dovetails perfectly with the tight-knit  group of characters under your control  and intimacy of the tactical maps on  which they're deployed there's a focus  and clarity of purpose here that stands  in sharp relief to the sprawling saga of  the typical XCOM campaign chimera score  feels like it spins the geo scope and  puts just one city under the microscope  nine districts comprised a strategic map  each day highlighting a few of them with  new missions to undertake some advancing  the story others providing valuable  resources and gear ignoring one mission  in favor of another will raise the level  of unrest in the neglected district and  contribute to a citywide measure of  Anarchy that spells game over if it tops  out despite the lower stakes there's  still an urgency to your missions  especially as the Doomsday Clock of city  Anarchy takes closer to midnight and the  strategic layer does a decent job of  forcing decisions over which missions  and districts you need to prioritize  even if it's mostly a dry game of  resource allocation that fails to evoke  any kind of emotional response to the  live sacrifice along the way chimera  squad performs better when bringing to  life the characters of the squad itself  elevators have been out for  so you get to take an alternate form of  transport to the top if you say the  water main I'll strangle you beyond the  initial gang of four there's a roster of  eleven named soldiers to be recruited  over the course of the game each of whom  boasts a unique set of abilities that  are unlocked as they gain experience and  I really mean unique no two abilities  are the same even when the broad roles  they perform are similar the specifics  cunningly conspired to demand separate  tactical considerations  across the cost of 11 characters with  seven abilities each five of which can  be earned in any one playthrough  that's an impressively diverse tactical  lineup one that succeeds in edging  distinct personality traits on each  soldier through the substantial  differences in their play styles adding  further character definition are the  clever ways a soldier's loadout is both  limited and given flexibility weapon use  is restricted to just one class but guns  can be outfitted with multiple mods and  on later missions you can discover  one-off named weapons that confer a  unique herbally on its owner missions  are designed around short sharp bursts  of action and often split into multiple  but no more than three encounters  confined to a handful of rooms that in  themselves are much smaller than any map  you'd find in recent ex-cons  the tactical choices start with the  breech for your decisions over who  enters by the main door the side window  the skylight the eminent hole in the  wall and so on have immediate  consequences here you're determining the  order in which your soldiers take their  turns in the initial phase the boss they  can apply and the positions they'll take  up once the fight gets underway all of  these choices are vital get them right  and you'll tip the balance in your favor  get them wrong and you'll have learned  some lessons for next time there's a  coarseness to the feel of the tactical  combat that contributes to the sense  every decision Mars actions are big and  definitive outside of the odd boss  encounter you're never whittling away at  an enemy's health bar shot steel damaged  by the wallop and you can down many  folds in one - or occasionally three  hits abilities can often be more  impactful than standard attacks to the  extent that I complete plenty of  missions without firing a regular shot  recognizing when the stars have aligned  for you to deploy that battle turning  herbally is an important tactical asset  being able to plan ahead and in the  space of a turn maneuver your squad and  the enemy into position to take full  advantage of that ability is invaluable  not to mention endlessly satisfying when  you pull it off  less satisfying is the addition of  androids to provide support to your  squad if while on a multi encounter  mission one squad member bleeds out  you're able to replace them with an  Android for the next encounter I played  through the campaign on the noir  difficulty and called upon a replacement  Android just once thus rendering the  resources I invested in upgrading them  pretty much wasted I needed their  service more frequently on the higher  difficulties but I couldn't shake the  nagging feeling that anything I invested  in backup groups would be better spent  on my collar squad on the occasions one  of my elite squad members went down the  untrained Android sent in their place  simply wasn't up to task there's an  uneasy balance here one that left me  feeling like I could only make poor  decisions and it's the one area where  the tweaks the XCOM formula aren't  obviously successful by stripping back  XCOM combat to its bare minimum chimera  squad is able to make nearly all 